      "text": "Let $N(x) = x^2 - 11x - 4.$ Then our equation turns into\n\\[N\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right) +N\\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right) + N\\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right) + N\\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right) = N(x).\\]If $x$ is a solution to this, then $-x$ must also be a solution. Moreover, $N(-x) = -N(x),$ because $N$ is symmetric. So we should eventually show that $\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right)^2 - 11\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right) - 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right)^2 - 11\\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right) - 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right)^2 - 11\\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right) - 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right)^2 - 11\\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right) - 4 = \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right)^2 + 11\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right) + 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right)^2 + 11\\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right) + 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right)^2 + 11\\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right) + 4 + \\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right)^2 + 11\\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right) + 4.$ Moreover, for $x \\neq 3, 5, 17, 19,$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{-1}{x-3}\\right)^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{x-3}^2\\\\\n\\left(\\dfrac{x-5}{x-5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{-1}{x-5}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{10}{x-5}^2\\\\\n\\left(\\dfrac{17}{x-17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-17}\\right)^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{x-17}^2\\\\\n\\left(\\dfrac{19}{x-19}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-19}\\right)^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{x-19}^2\n\\end{align*}Substituting and simplifying, we get\n\\[\\dfrac{10}{x-3}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-5}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-17}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-19}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-89}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-99}^2 = 181x + 1087\\]But the left side is symmetric, so\n\\[\\dfrac{10}{x-89}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-99}^2 = 181x + 1087.\\]Similarly we have $\\dfrac{10}{x-89}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-99}^2 = \\dfrac{10}{x-89}^2 + \\dfrac{10}{x-99}^2 - 2 = 61x + 177,$ and both of the sides are equal to $83x+92$. So\n\\[\\dfrac{10x-920}{x^2 - 178x + 1941} = 83x + 92.\\]The numerator must equal zero. We get $x = \\dfrac{4715+40\\sqrt{2841}}{3078}.$. However, this is not largest. So we can ignore it.\nWe can just raise both sides to the power of $3$ and get\n\\[x^2 + 11x = mx\\]Expanding, we get $x^2 - (m+11)x = 0$. Hence, $(x-11)(x-m) = 0$. The largest real solution occurs when $x = m + 11$, so our answer is $\\boxed{5}.$. The answer is 5",
